WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.626
 ♪  

00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:05.880
 CHIP: When you're providing 
 things, you're doing things, 

00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:08.133
 it's so much easier when 
 you're done with the work 

00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.718
 to sit back and enjoy 

00:00:09.759 --> 00:00:11.678
 than to spend 
 a whole day doing nothing. 

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.558
 ♪  

00:00:16.599 --> 00:00:21.062
 ♪  

00:00:21.104 --> 00:00:23.356
 CAROL: Come to me, 
 fishy, fishy. 

00:00:23.398 --> 00:00:25.692
 WADE: That sounded 
 new to me, too. 

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:27.110
 AGNES: No contact. 

00:00:27.152 --> 00:00:30.113
 CHIP: Look at that. 
 I caught leaves. 

00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:31.781
 (laughs) 

00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:34.242
 Today, we're fishing down 
 in a place called O'lokatok. 

00:00:34.284 --> 00:00:35.827
 My wife assures me 
 it's a good place. 

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:38.413
 As a kid, she used to 
 go down with her sister. 

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:41.207
 We eat a lot of fish, especially 
 with the decline of the caribou, 

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:43.334
 and we need some dog food, 
 and we need some people food. 

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:45.420
 This time of year, we have 
 two kinds of fish here. 

00:00:45.462 --> 00:00:47.047
 There are sheefish 
 and there's the pike 

00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:48.381
 that are out 
 to get the sheefish. 

00:00:48.423 --> 00:00:49.549
 Well, I intend 
 to catch them both, 

00:00:49.591 --> 00:00:52.177
 so, yeah, we'll see 
 how that goes. 

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:55.180
 WADE: Why do we have to do this? 

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.224
 CHIP: So we can eat the fish. 
 If you don't eat, you go hungry. 

00:00:58.266 --> 00:00:59.809
 You know when 
 the caribou are gone 

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:02.187
 and the rabbits are gone, 
 ptarmigan are down. 

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.063
 WADE: Just fish. 

00:01:03.104 --> 00:01:04.314
 CHIP: Fishing 
 is still pretty good. 

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:07.067
 Like hunting, do you like 
 to hunt for your family? 

00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:07.901
 WADE: Yeah. 

00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:09.319
 CHIP: You can fish 
 for your family. 

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.072
 You can get your anaa fish. 
 You can get your mom fish. 

00:01:12.113 --> 00:01:13.531
 You can get 
 all your aunties fish. 

00:01:13.531 --> 00:01:15.366
 You can get your uncle 
 some fish. 

00:01:15.366 --> 00:01:16.910
 WADE: Hope I get one. 

00:01:16.951 --> 00:01:18.203
 CHIP: I hope so, too. 

00:01:18.203 --> 00:01:21.748
 AGNES: Chip and I decided 
 to take Wade Kelly out fishing. 

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:24.000
 Bringing the kid out 
 to spend the night camping 

00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:25.919
 is really important 
 while he's young 

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.545
 so he'll want to 
 go back out there, 

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:31.299
 get more fish, and provide 
 for his family. 

00:01:31.341 --> 00:01:32.634
 CHIP: Just 'cause the fish 
 aren't here right now 

00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:34.010
 doesn't mean they 
 won't be here later, 

00:01:34.052 --> 00:01:35.970
 so you can always 
 try these holes again. 

00:01:36.012 --> 00:01:37.388
 Another thing, 
 we can go put holes 

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:40.391
 all the way along the river 
 if we felt like it. 

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:42.602
 AGNES: Could go a little bit 
 farther out still. 

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:45.396
 CHIP: Yeah. 'Cause I'm, 
 I've got about that much water 

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:46.773
 between the ice. 

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:48.316
 AGNES: Oh, I caught one! 

00:01:48.358 --> 00:01:51.069
 CHIP: Whoa, whoa, sheefish! 

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:52.070
 AGNES: A little one. 

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:52.946
 CHIP: Sheefish, sheefish. 

00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:54.697
 AGNES: Yay! 

00:01:54.739 --> 00:01:57.033
 (Chip laughs) 

00:01:57.075 --> 00:01:57.909
 Yes! 

00:01:57.909 --> 00:01:59.911
 WADE: Whip it! 
 Are you gonna whip it? 

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.162
 AGNES: I got it already. 

00:02:01.204 --> 00:02:03.915
 CHIP: See, Grandson. 
 Here, you trade me holes. 

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:07.794
 WADE: Lucky my anaa catch one. 

00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:11.381
 We're gonna 
 have fish for dinner. 

00:02:11.422 --> 00:02:13.091
 AGNES: Oh, I caught one! 

00:02:13.091 --> 00:02:15.301
 WADE: She catch two of 'em! 

00:02:15.343 --> 00:02:18.263
 CHIP: Yeah. Your anaa's 
 a good fisherwoman. 

00:02:18.263 --> 00:02:21.099
 AGNES: (laughs) 

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:24.227
 No, I just was thinking-- 
 oh, I got one! 

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:26.604
 CHIP: Again? Man! 

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:29.691
 Three different holes, 
 at that. Gee whiz! 

00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:32.068
 AGNES: Every hole! 

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.487
 CHIP: Wow, just go over here 
 and bless mine, would ya? 

00:02:34.529 --> 00:02:36.406
 Wow! 

00:02:36.447 --> 00:02:39.284
 She is fishing 
 circles around us, 

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:41.536
 literally, look at this. 

00:02:41.578 --> 00:02:42.745
 Right around us. 

00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:46.624
 WADE: We got three fish now 
 because of my anaa. 

00:02:46.624 --> 00:02:47.500
 CAROL: Got one. 

00:02:47.542 --> 00:02:48.293
 WADE: What? 

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.335
 CHIP: Oh, a big one? 

00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:52.338
 WADE: Just leave a fish for me. 

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.756
 AGNES: Oh, I caught one, too! 

00:02:53.798 --> 00:02:55.550
 CAROL: Pull it! 
 Oh, man, you guys. 

00:02:55.592 --> 00:02:57.468
 AGNES: I got a siulik! 

00:02:57.468 --> 00:03:00.263
 CHIP: Oh, me, too! 
 Oh, me, too, look at that. 

00:03:00.305 --> 00:03:02.807
 Oh, I got a pike, too. 
 Woo-hoo! 

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.809
 Wow, two siuliks at once. 

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:07.312
 CAROL: What are you guys doing? 
 (laughs) 

00:03:07.312 --> 00:03:08.938
 WADE: I hope I get one. 

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:12.734
 AGNES: Grandson, the trick 
 is not to let your line loose. 

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:15.153
 Always keep it tight 
 like this. Look, tight. 

00:03:15.153 --> 00:03:16.863
 And your fish won't get away. 

00:03:16.905 --> 00:03:19.157
 CHIP: You'll learn, Grandson. 

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:20.491
 Ooh, I got one. 

00:03:20.491 --> 00:03:21.326
 AGNES: Hey, hey! 

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:23.328
 WADE: A big one, 
 bigger than all of them. 

00:03:23.328 --> 00:03:27.123
 AGNES: Hey, right on, 
 Carol, woo-hoo! 

00:03:27.165 --> 00:03:31.544
 WADE: Oh, she finally got one. 

00:03:31.586 --> 00:03:32.921
 Oh, I got one! 

00:03:32.962 --> 00:03:34.464
 CHIP: Keep going, 
 Grandson, keep going. 

00:03:36.507 --> 00:03:38.009
 CHIP: Oh, he got a fish stick! 

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:42.805
 (laughing) All right! 

00:03:42.847 --> 00:03:45.516
 Grandson, come over here. 
 I'll make a new hole for me. 

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:46.768
 Come on, you can 
 come fish further, 

00:03:46.809 --> 00:03:49.520
 where you can catch 
 something. Come on. 

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:54.484
 WADE: I thought I got one, 
 but it was just that stick. 

00:03:54.525 --> 00:03:56.819
 AGNES: It's really important 
 to, um, make sure 

00:03:56.861 --> 00:03:58.738
 we keep trying 
 until he catches a fish, 

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:03.034
 and that way he'll get that 
 feeling of, you know, providing, 

00:04:03.034 --> 00:04:05.745
 and that's very important 
 that he gets that 

00:04:05.787 --> 00:04:08.790
 if he wants to be a hunter 
 when he grows up. 

00:04:08.831 --> 00:04:12.001
 WADE: I just want 
 to catch one fish! 

00:04:12.043 --> 00:04:14.379
 ♪  

